  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only adapter must implement TermLike's horizontal\n movement callback. Indicatif's single-line renderer never calls it;\n the no-op does not participate in application behavior. The PTY\n integration test exercises the actual terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is an explicit function with a body, which is a permitted target. Its fenced commentary gives a substantive reason for the exception, not just difficulty.\n\n- **Why the function exists:** `move_cursor_right` is a required method of `TermLike`. The test-only adapter `RecordingTerm` must provide it, and the body is a bare `Ok(())` no-op.\n- **Why it goes unexecuted:** The commentary says indicatif's single-line renderer never calls it. I couldn't read the indicatif source, so I'm relying on my recollection of its draw code, which uses `move_cursor_up`, `clear_line` and `write_line`. The cursor-movement methods the tests do use are separate items.\n- **Where the real behavior is tested:** The PTY integration test `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` exists. It runs the actual terminal path that `Progress::new` builds with `console::Term`, so the commentary's claim about it agrees with the source.\n\nThe exception covers only this function's unexecuted state and does not excuse the file's threshold.",
